    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `PydanticPlugin._pydantic_model_metaclass_marker_callback`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_pydantic_model_metaclass_marker_callback` function is a method within the `PydanticPlugin` class that resets the `dataclass_transform_spec` attribute of the `ModelMetaclass`. This function is designed to ensure that the Pydantic plugin handles the metaclass transformation, rather than relying on the default behavior. It accepts a single parameter, `ctx`, which is an instance of `ClassDefContext` representing the class definition context. The function first checks if the `debug_dataclass_transform` configuration option is set to `True`. If it is, the function returns immediately without making any changes. Otherwise, it retrieves the declared metaclass from the class definition context and asserts that it is not `None`, indicating that the callback was properly passed from the `get_metaclass_hook`. If the `dataclass_transform_spec` attribute exists on the metaclass type, it is reset to `None`. This function does not return any value and has no side effects other than modifying the `dataclass_transform_spec` attribute of the metaclass. It relies on the `assert` statement to handle the case where the metaclass is `None`, which would indicate an error in the callback mechanism.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what i